WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:03.699
In 1648, the Water Board of
Lekdijk Bovendams

00:00:03.699 --> 00:00:05.906
needed to repair these
embankments to make sure

00:00:05.906 --> 00:00:07.834
that this bit of the
Netherlands didn't flood.

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:09.306
For that, they needed money,

00:00:09.306 --> 00:00:11.311
so they sold a bearer bond.

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.825
Whoever owned that bond would get

00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:15.401
a little bit of money
back from them every year.

00:00:15.401 --> 00:00:17.199
But, unlike modern bonds, they didn't set

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:18.914
a time limit on that.

00:00:18.914 --> 00:00:20.874
Perpetual debt can be a thing in finance.

00:00:20.874 --> 00:00:23.120
It's kind of like selling
stock in a company,

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.005
but normally perpetual just means

00:00:25.005 --> 00:00:27.397
until the company fails.

00:00:27.397 --> 00:00:29.569
But, this company didn't fail.

00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:33.736
3½ centuries later, that
original bond ended up....

00:00:35.741 --> 00:00:37.417
GPS: "Take exit 47."

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.680
...here, at Yale University in
New Haven, Connecticut, USA,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.640
and it's still paying interest.

00:00:43.648 --> 00:00:46.715
Perpetual bonds date
back to the Middle Ages.

00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:48.928
All bonds were perpetual bonds

00:00:48.928 --> 00:00:51.396
and the fact that bonds actually mature

00:00:51.396 --> 00:00:54.381
and pay back their principal
is a financial innovation.

00:00:54.381 --> 00:00:57.260
The flood plains of rivers,
they don't coincide

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.361
with state boundaries or city boundaries,

00:01:00.361 --> 00:01:01.710
so they had separate governments

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.469
that would oversee the levees.

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:06.613
These organisations would
have the power of taxation.

00:01:06.613 --> 00:01:09.145
Everybody who was protected by a levee

00:01:09.145 --> 00:01:12.976
would pay taxes to pay for their upkeep.

00:01:12.976 --> 00:01:14.406
And they would never go to war,

00:01:14.406 --> 00:01:15.512
so the combination of being able

00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:17.392
to tax people and not going to war,

00:01:17.392 --> 00:01:19.433
which usually bankrupts governments,

00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:20.763
makes that these bonds

00:01:20.763 --> 00:01:22.411
are still paying interest until today.

00:01:22.411 --> 00:01:25.482
Tax collections would have
fell short of the budget they needed,

00:01:25.482 --> 00:01:27.605
so they issued this bond.

00:01:27.605 --> 00:01:31.532
The water board, which
dates back to, I think, 1328

00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:34.558
has been merged in larger
and larger organisations.

00:01:34.558 --> 00:01:36.969
They have recombined these water boards

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:40.045
and now it's called Stichtse Rijnlanden

00:01:40.045 --> 00:01:41.675
which pays the interest on the bond.

00:01:41.675 --> 00:01:44.793
These annotations here are
the annual interest payments

00:01:44.793 --> 00:01:48.415
that have been taking place since 1648.

00:01:48.415 --> 00:01:51.076
As you go to the other side,

00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:53.117
you see that the whole other side

00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:55.372
is also full with little markings

00:01:55.372 --> 00:01:56.806
which are the interest payments

00:01:56.806 --> 00:01:58.399
that have taken back over the years.

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:00.038
At some point, the bond was full.

00:02:00.038 --> 00:02:01.430
There was no more space,

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:04.182
and they issued this piece of paper here

00:02:04.182 --> 00:02:07.003
which is a continued marking
of the interest payments.

00:02:07.003 --> 00:02:10.313
When Yale bought the bond in 2003,

00:02:10.313 --> 00:02:11.868
and we collected our interest payment,

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:13.218
you see that the last interest payment

00:02:13.218 --> 00:02:15.956
had been taken place in 1976.

00:02:15.956 --> 00:02:20.123
So, there was 27 years of
back interest that was due.

00:02:20.127 --> 00:02:22.582
We left the bond here for 12 years,

00:02:22.582 --> 00:02:28.020
and then, in 2015, we
collected '04, '05, '06,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.836
all the way down to 2015.

00:02:30.836 --> 00:02:32.102
Well, there was actually
a picture somewhere

00:02:32.102 --> 00:02:35.701
of me in Holland where I
hold the bond in one hand

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:37.707
and I got interest payments for Yale

00:02:37.707 --> 00:02:41.145
with my other hand as
the bearer of the bond.

00:02:41.145 --> 00:02:42.416
It's a little disappointing.

00:02:42.416 --> 00:02:46.583
The bond initially was for
1,000 carolus guilders.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.480
Carolus guilders became
other guilders, I think.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.116
They became, now, Euros.

00:02:52.116 --> 00:02:55.636
At some point, they lowered
the interest payments a bit.

00:02:55.636 --> 00:02:57.512
So, to make a long story short,

00:02:57.512 --> 00:03:00.860
every year, we get €11.35

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.820
interest payment on this bond.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.414
You know, the value of this bond,

00:03:04.414 --> 00:03:07.274
obviously, is in the fact that it's live.

00:03:07.274 --> 00:03:08.912
It's probably just as exciting for them

00:03:08.912 --> 00:03:11.808
as it is for us to be
able to present the bond

00:03:11.808 --> 00:03:13.777
and have an opportunity to pay interest.

00:03:13.777 --> 00:03:15.875
Now, the Dutch water authority
could probably default

00:03:15.875 --> 00:03:17.737
on the debt if they wanted to.

00:03:17.737 --> 00:03:19.147
It costs more than the bond is worth

00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:21.813
to fly someone over
every couple of decades.

00:03:21.813 --> 00:03:24.022
And let's face it, Yale would be unlikely

00:03:24.022 --> 00:03:26.306
to launch an international lawsuit.

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:29.017
But, for about one Euro a month,

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.408
the Water Authority gets to be part

00:03:30.408 --> 00:03:33.840
of a weird bit of living
financial history,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.900
and get a bit of good publicity
out of it too.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.240
It might not be a subscription
they'd take out deliberately,

00:03:39.248 --> 00:03:42.026
but apparently, for them, it's worth it.

00:03:42.026 --> 00:03:44.722
I think they have a
pretty good track record.

00:03:44.722 --> 00:03:47.486
They sound like a triple-A
organisation to me,

00:03:47.486 --> 00:03:51.403
and I think we're going
to be good for a while.

